 | 10 | Strict warnings without warnings and regular static analysis. |
 
-Rule 5 has two published formulations: the 2006 paper and [overview](https://spinroot.com/p10/) use an average, while the author's [detailed Rule 5](https://spinroot.com/p10/rule5.html) uses N assertions in functions longer than M lines, with N=2/M=20 for safety-critical code. Following the user's 2026-10-01 choice, retain the project's strict N=2/M=20 gate and also report any global density with an explicit denominator and counting limitations. The historical per-function findings are valid strict-gate findings, not by themselves proof of violating the paper's average. Never use an average to excuse a missing safety-critical contract or manufacture assertions to meet either formulation.
+Rule 5 has two published formulations: the 2006 paper and [overview](https://spinroot.com/p10/) use an average, while the author's [detailed Rule 5](https://spinroot.com/p10/rule5.html) uses N assertions in functions longer than M lines. Following the user's revised 2026-10-01 06:31 UTC decision, the acceptance gate is an average of at least two meaningful runtime assertions per function in each target's declared first-party scan. Flight and Ground must report their own numerator, denominator, configuration and result. Include short and zero-assertion functions in the denominator. Long functions with fewer than two assertions are nonblocking review diagnostics, not current Rule 5 errors. Preserve the historical N=2/M=20 reports with their original scope and date; do not rewrite them as passes. Never use an average to excuse a missing safety-critical contract or manufacture assertions.
 
-The existing checker is a project gate, not a complete proof of these rules. Preserve its original thresholds, source coverage and failure expectations. In particular, its Rule 5 requirement of two assertions in functions over 20 code lines differs from the original average across functions; typed-object non-null/alignment counting does not prove useful assertion density. Its direct-recursion/text-pattern checks do not establish an acyclic whole-program call graph, finite `for` bounds, minimum data scope, every checked return, or all header/macro/pointer semantics. Review those separately and record unresolved items as FAIL or NOT PROVEN, never PASS.
+The checker is a project gate, not a complete proof of these rules. Preserve the other nine rules' thresholds, source coverage, exceptions and failure expectations. Its syntactically eligible assertion count is an upper bound pending semantic review: constants, obvious tautologies, repeated predicates, fixed local/static addresses and natural alignment of typed objects earn no density credit. Unknown semantic validity is NOT PROVEN; a tool PASS alone does not certify useful assertion density. Direct-recursion/text-pattern checks do not establish an acyclic whole-program call graph, finite `for` bounds, minimum data scope, every checked return, or all header/macro/pointer semantics. Review those separately and record unresolved items as FAIL or NOT PROVEN, never PASS.
 
 Known project policies also differ from strict original compliance: the checker forbids allocation even during initialization, excludes third-party middleware, permits its named permanent task/fault loops, selected protocol/estimator conditionals and the exact FreeRTOS idle-hook double-pointer outputs. The existing [coding standard](docs/FCCG/platform/details/CODING_STANDARD.md) also records fixed task entry pointers passed directly to `xTaskCreateStatic()`; this does not authorize a runtime callback registry or further pointer indirection. Do not enlarge these exceptions without explicit authorization. Maintain upstream licenses and version provenance. Inventory linked third-party code (including HAL, RTOS, libraries and callbacks), document each controlled exception and its consequences for recursion, stack and timing analysis; do not erase its violations or claim whole-firmware original compliance from a first-party gate alone. Host fault-injection harnesses may intercept the terminal trap using setjmp/longjmp; this test-only mechanism must never enter target firmware or be reported as MCU recovery.
 
-Assertions must enforce a real input, stored-state, sequence, capacity, numerical or lifecycle contract at the relevant boundary, before unsafe access or partial publication. Invalid external input should retain the intended ordinary error path. Corrupt internal state must enter the established bounded safe failure/fail-stop path and must not leave stale READY or publish a successful result. Respect task/ISR ownership and critical sections; an assertion based on an incoherent concurrent snapshot is invalid. Do not add constant/tautological assertions, repeat the same check for counts, split or reformat functions merely to evade a threshold, broaden allowlists, hide files, suppress diagnostics, weaken checks, or change tests to turn a genuine failure green. If two meaningful assertions cannot be justified, retain and report the gap instead of manufacturing compliance.
+Assertions must enforce a real input, stored-state, sequence, capacity, numerical or lifecycle contract at the relevant boundary, before unsafe access or partial publication. Invalid external input should retain the intended ordinary error path. Corrupt internal state must enter the established bounded safe failure/fail-stop path and must not leave stale READY or publish a successful result. Respect task/ISR ownership and critical sections; an assertion based on an incoherent concurrent snapshot is invalid. Do not add constant/tautological assertions, repeat the same check for counts, split or reformat functions merely to evade a threshold, broaden allowlists, hide files, suppress diagnostics, weaken checks, or change tests to turn a genuine failure green. If a meaningful safety contract cannot be justified, explain the uncertainty; do not manufacture assertions for density or per-function diagnostics.
 
-Each safety batch must demonstrate the pre-fix failure and post-fix normal and fault-triggering cases with unchanged safety expectations. Run the unmodified generated `power10-check`, relevant Host regressions, real static-analysis compilation (not syntax-only masquerading as analyzer execution), and actual ARM compilation/linking. Check source-graph/architecture and linked artifact/stack reports when affected. Save commands, tool versions, exact source commit or auditable dirty snapshot, configuration, exit codes, complete failure/skip details, logs and artifact hashes. Use fresh outputs; never attribute an old binary or a result from another revision to a fix. Do not stitch selected passes into an unexecuted full-suite pass.
+Each safety batch must demonstrate the pre-fix failure and post-fix normal and fault-triggering cases with unchanged safety expectations. Run the generated `power10-check` exactly as emitted by the owning template (record authorized gate revisions), relevant Host regressions, real static-analysis compilation (not syntax-only masquerading as analyzer execution), and actual ARM compilation/linking. Check source-graph/architecture and linked artifact/stack reports when affected. Save commands, tool versions, exact source commit or auditable dirty snapshot, configuration, exit codes, complete failure/skip details, logs and artifact hashes. Use fresh outputs; never attribute an old binary or a result from another revision to a fix. Do not stitch selected passes into an unexecuted full-suite pass.
 
 Any failed gate blocks a claim of compliance. Passing tools establish only their recorded scope. A stack proof must cover actual task paths, libraries, indirect calls, dynamic frames, main/MSP and relevant ISR nesting; unresolved bounds remain unproven. Linked RAM/CCM spare bytes, host execution time and an assertion count do not establish safe stack margin, STM32 WCET, navigation accuracy or hardware/flight qualification. Keep existing fault behavior and record unavailable hardware evidence explicitly. Do not perform hardware actions, publish or push without the user's authorization.
